    explanation: "Knowing how much rent you can comfortably afford is the first step in any apartment search, and it prevents the painful mistake of falling for a place that quietly strains your budget every month. A rent affordability calculator turns your annual income and a chosen rent-to-income percentage into a clear monthly ceiling, so you can filter listings with confidence and negotiate leases knowing exactly where your limit sits.\n\nThe widely cited rule of thumb is to spend no more than thirty percent of gross income on rent, a guideline landlords themselves often use when screening applicants. The calculator converts your yearly income to a monthly figure and applies your percentage, producing the maximum rent that keeps housing in balance with the rest of your life. If you carry heavy debt or want to save aggressively, you can dial the percentage down; if you live in an expensive city and prioritise location, you might stretch it up, eyes open to the trade-off.\n\nThe math works because rent is best judged as a proportion of income rather than an absolute number: fifteen hundred dollars is comfortable on one salary and crushing on another. Framing affordability as a share keeps the decision grounded in your actual earnings. Treat the result as a ceiling, not a target, and remember that utilities, renters insurance, and other living costs sit on top of rent, so leaving headroom below the maximum is usually the wiser move.",
    example: { inputs: { annualIncome: 60000, rentToIncomePercent: 30 }, explanation: "On a $60,000 salary, monthly income is $5,000. Applying the 30% rule gives a maximum rent of $5,000 × 0.30 = $1,500 per month before utilities and other costs." },
