    explanation: "A mortgage payoff calculator answers two questions every borrower eventually asks: how long until this loan is gone, and how much interest will it cost me along the way? By entering your current balance, your monthly payment, and your interest rate, you get the number of months remaining until the balance hits zero, plus the total interest you will hand the lender before you are free of the debt.\n\nThe result is powerful because it makes the effect of your payment size tangible. Every dollar above the minimum goes straight to principal, and because interest is charged on the shrinking balance, paying a little extra each month can shave years off the loan and save a striking amount of interest. Running the numbers at your current payment, then at a higher one, shows exactly how much time and money accelerating your payoff would buy.\n\nThe calculation works by inverting the standard amortization formula. Normally that formula sets a payment for a fixed term; here we know the payment and solve for the term instead, using a logarithm to find how many months of payments drive the balance to zero. One important guardrail: if your monthly payment does not exceed the interest accruing each month, the balance never falls and the loan can never be paid off, so the calculator returns no result in that case to flag an impossible scenario.",
    example: { inputs: { currentBalance: 200000, monthlyPayment: 1200, annualRate: 5 }, explanation: "A $200,000 balance at 5% paid down at $1,200 a month takes about 285 months, roughly 24 years, to clear. Over that time you pay about $142,170 in total interest on top of the principal." },
