    explanation: "Return on investment, or ROI, is the plain-language way most people judge whether a rental property is worth owning. A rental property ROI calculator subtracts the annual expenses from the annual rental income to find the yearly cash flow, then divides that by the purchase price to express the return as a percentage. In one figure it tells you how hard the property's price is working to generate income.\n\nThe measure is popular because it is intuitive and easy to compare. A property returning eight percent on its price is producing more income per dollar than one returning four percent, all else equal. Framing the return against the full purchase price gives an unleveraged view, treating the property as if you paid all cash, which strips out the distortions of different financing arrangements and lets you compare deals on the asset's own merits.\n\nThe calculation works because it ties recurring net income directly to the cost of acquiring the asset, the same logic behind a bond's yield. It does have limits: by using purchase price rather than the cash you actually put down, it does not capture the amplifying effect of a mortgage, and by looking at a single year it ignores appreciation and loan paydown. Even so, it is an excellent first-pass screen. Pair it with cash-on-cash return for the leveraged view and cap rate for market comparison, and you have a rounded picture of a rental's performance.",
